window.docContents[4].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Classical-reconstruction-and-solution-operators":{"contents":"Under coherent data at every Sobolev order and operationally smooth boundary\ncharts, the principal weak solution has one representative in\n. The proof raises each finite Sobolev order to a\nsupercritical  jet, reconstructs the classical derivatives\nwithin the closure using boundary charts and derivative extension, and then\nidentifies the finite-order representatives by almost-everywhere agreement\nand continuity. The interior  theorem is the\ncorresponding restriction.\n\nUniqueness and the closed graph theorem turn the principal regularity\ntheorems into bounded linear maps\n\n\n\nTheir norm inequalities use the operator norm, so the constant is uniform in\nthe forcing datum but is not given explicitly in terms of the coefficients or\nthe domain. These maps come from the principal\ndivergence-form solver. For the full operator with first- and zeroth-order\nterms, at any shift above the Gårding threshold, the same closed-graph argument\ngives a bounded solution map  whose values solve the shifted\nweak equation with zero Dirichlet trace.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Regularity and geometry","header":"5.7.1.3.4. Classical reconstruction and solution operators","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Classical-reconstruction-and-solution-operators"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Diffusion-and-weak-evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--The-kernel___s-derivative___-step-by-step":{"contents":"The kernel time derivative below uses positivity of  when\ndifferentiating its real power. The calculation applies to the Gaussian\nbranch at positive time.\n\nopen PDE.Common HeatEquation in\nexample {n : ℕ} (x : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ) (ht : t > 0) :\n    deriv (fun s ↦ fundamentalSolutionHeat (n := n) x s) t =\n      (‖x‖ ^ 2 / (4 * t ^ 2) - (n : ℝ) / (2 * t)) *\n        fundamentalSolutionHeat (n := n) x t := by\n  have hgt_pos : 0 < 4 * Real.pi * t := by positivity\n  -- Work with the `t > 0` branch of the definition via\n  -- an eventual equality near `t`.\n  have hbranch :\n      (fun s ↦ fundamentalSolutionHeat (n := n) x s) =ᶠ[nhds t]\n        (fun s ↦\n          (4 * Real.pi * s) ^ (-(n : ℝ) / 2) *\n            Real.exp (-(‖x‖ ^ 2 / (4 * s)))) := by\n    filter_upwards [eventually_gt_nhds ht] with s hs\n    simp [fundamentalSolutionHeat, not_le_of_gt hs, mul_comm]\n  rw [hbranch.deriv_eq, fundamentalSolutionHeat, ite_eq_right (not_le_of_gt ht)]\n  cas using hgt_pos\n\n\nThe squared heat-ball slice radius is\n. Its derivative calculation uses\n and  to differentiate the logarithm.\n\nopen HeatEquation in\nexample (n : ℕ) {r τ : ℝ} (hr : r ≠ 0) (hτ : τ ≠ 0) :\n    HasDerivAt (heatBallSliceRadiusSq n r)\n      (2 * (n : ℝ) * (Real.log (r ^ 2 / (4 * Real.pi * τ)) - 1)) τ := by\n  unfold heatBallSliceRadiusSq\n  cas\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Diffusion and weak evolution","header":"6.1.3. The kernel's derivative, step by step","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Diffusion-and-weak-evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--The-kernel___s-derivative___-step-by-step"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Choosing-an-Interface":{"contents":"Most users should stay in ordinary Lean notation and choose an interface by\nwhat they are trying to do:\n\n* Use #cas [...] expression while exploring. It calculates and prints the\ninferred domain and result, registering auxiliary theorems for its proof\nevidence.* Use bare cas, or cas using h with a domain hypothesis, when an ordinary\ntheorem goal should be closed by algebraic reasoning, checked\ndifferentiation, or a supported integral rule.* Use cas_nf to transform a supported real expression inside a larger goal\nor hypothesis, or inside a selected conv occurrence. The reflected\nVerified.Expr.eval interface remains available.* Use cega using certificate when a proof already has an enclosure, error,\nquadrature, or residual certificate and needs its sound theorem. Bare\ncega projects a matching checked certificate or infers a rational box from\nlocal hypotheses.* Use cega [enclose x in [a, b]] e when an EnclosedOn, BoundOn, or\nApproxOn goal over an exact rational box, or an ordinary inequality in the\nbox variables, should be closed by a fresh interval certificate; the goal\nselects the projection. Prefix (prec := n) or (depth := n) when the\ndefault policy is too coarse.* Use cas? or cega? when the same proof search should also explain the\nselected route in the InfoView.* Use bare cega for a numerical bound on an interval integral with a closed real\nintegrand and rational limits. It accounts for the quadrature error as well as sample\nuncertainty.* Use the lower-level Verified.Expr and certificate constructors only when\nextending the engine or building a reusable verified algorithm.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.1. Choosing an Interface","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Choosing-an-Interface"}});